Actions from recent postmortems that change on-call. They are tracked as tickets, and the list here is updated at each runbook review.

- [x] Remove the automatic failover code path from the router — @tomas-veidt, :priority[P1], :due[2026-06-30]
- [ ] Add an alert for bank transfers stuck in `submitted` for more than one batch — @amara-quist, :priority[P2], :due[2026-11-15]
- [ ] Raise the lead time of `CertExpirySoon` for client certificates from 14 to 30 days — @lena-marsh, :priority[P2], :due[2026-11-20]
- [ ] Add the Coralpay duty manager escalation number to the acquirer contact sheet — @kofi-annerud, :priority[P3], :due[2026-11-10]
- [ ] Write the playbook for disputes and chargeback spikes — @priya-nandakumar, :priority[P3], :due[2026-12-15]

## Limits reference

The limits below come up often during incidents. Most of them are set in configuration and some are set by partners. The table says which, so you know whether a limit can move.

| Limit | Value | Set by | Can on-call change it |
|-|-|-|-|
| Merchant request rate at the edge | 1,000 requests per second per merchant | Rail configuration | No, Rail Core only |
| Request rate for platform merchants | 5,000 requests per second per merchant | Rail configuration | No, Rail Core only |
| Idempotency key retention | 24 hours | Rail design | No |
| Authorization timeout to acquirer | 6 seconds | Rail configuration | No |
| Capture and refund timeout to acquirer | 10 seconds | Rail configuration | No |
| Northgate request rate from Paywise | 3,000 per second, both regions together | Northgate contract | No |
| Coralpay request rate from Paywise | 1,500 per second, both regions together | Coralpay contract | No |
| Connections per acquirer connector pod | 200 | Rail configuration | No |
| Router pods per region | 30 at most | Northgate connection limit | Scale within it |
| Edge and dispatcher pods per region | 40 at most | Rail configuration | Scale within it |
| Fraud scoring budget per call | 120 ms | Risk Engineering | No |
| Fail-open duration per command | 60 minutes | Rail design | Renew, not extend |
| Webhook retries | 16 over 72 hours | Rail design | No |
| Largest single refund | The captured amount of the payment, never more | Card network rules | No |
| Bank transfer second approval | Above 100,000 EUR | Finance policy | No |

A merchant who hits the edge rate limit gets HTTP 429 with a `Retry-After` header. That is working as intended, and it is not an incident unless the merchant is a platform merchant who has been mistakenly left on the standard limit. The platform merchant list is in the edge configuration and has 23 entries.

Refunds can be split, so one capture can have several partial refunds, but together they can never exceed the captured amount. A refund request that would go over is rejected with `refund_exceeds_capture` before it reaches the acquirer. Refunds older than 180 days after the capture are not supported by either acquirer. Merchants must pay those customers back outside Rail.

## Error codes merchants see

Merchants and support often quote an error code. These are the ones that come up during incidents, with what they usually mean for on-call.

| Code | HTTP status | What happened | Usually means |
|-|-|-|-|
| `acquirer_timeout` | 504 | The acquirer did not answer in time | Acquirer problem or our network path, payment state unknown |
| `acquirer_capacity` | 503 | Our client-side limiter rejected the request | Too much traffic for one acquirer, often after a weight change |
| `service_unavailable` | 503 | A dependency is down, often the vault breaker | Vault or region problem |
| `capture_blocked` | 409 | Captures for this merchant are blocked by on-call | An open double charge incident |
| `idempotency_mismatch` | 409 | Same idempotency key, different body | Merchant bug, not an incident |
| `refund_exceeds_capture` | 422 | Refunds would total more than the capture | Merchant bug or a refund sent twice |
| `rate_limited` | 429 | Merchant over their edge rate limit | Working as intended, check the platform list |
| `fraud_declined` | 402 | Fraud scoring or the fallback rule declined it | Normal, unless it spikes during fail-open |

The error message text that goes with each code is written for merchants' developers, and it never says which acquirer was involved. When a merchant asks which acquirer handled a payment, support can tell them through the dashboard's payment detail page, which shows it for their own payments only.

A spike in `fraud_declined` during fail-open means a merchant's fallback rule is "decline all" or has a low limit. That is the rule working as agreed with the merchant, and on-call does not change it. Tell the merchant's account manager, so they can talk to Risk Engineering on the next business day.

## Merchant exceptions

Some merchants have arrangements that change how a playbook applies to them. Check this list when an incident is limited to one merchant.

- Merchants pinned to one acquirer by contract do not fail over and are not moved by `set-weights`. There are 41 of them, listed under `pinned_merchants`. Their account managers must be told when their acquirer has problems.
- Merchants on the cascading addendum are retried on the second acquirer for decline code 91 only. There are 6 of them.
- Merchants on the legacy webhook format do not use the slow lane. There are 14 of them.
- Charities on the reduced-fee programme settle once a week instead of daily, on Mondays. A missing settlement file for them on other days is expected.
- Two marketplace merchants split each payment between many sellers. Their captures carry up to 50 split instructions each, and their capture calls take up to 4 seconds even when everything is healthy. Do not count their slow captures towards latency incidents.

Merchants with a dedicated support plan have a named technical contact at Paywise. For those merchants, the account manager joins every SEV1 or SEV2 that affects them, and the communications lead adds them to the incident channel.

## Daily checks

The primary does these checks every weekday morning before 10:00 UTC, and on weekends when convenient. They take about 20 minutes on a quiet day and catch most slow-burning problems before they page anyone.

1. Settlement. Confirm that last night's settlement files were uploaded to both acquirers and that both acquirers have acknowledged them. The acknowledgement for Northgate arrives within an hour of upload, Coralpay's can take until 08:00 UTC.
2. Reconciliation report. Open the overnight reconciliation report from Payments Ledger. Any line in the "unmatched captures" section older than 2 days goes to the Payments Ledger team as a ticket. Do not try to fix unmatched captures yourself.
3. Webhook failures. Check the list of merchants with more than 1,000 failed webhooks in the last 24 hours. Tell their account managers through the shared queue, so the merchant can fix their endpoint before they miss more events.
4. Certificates. Look at the certificate panel for anything expiring within 30 days, even though the alert only fires at 14.
5. Silences. Review every active alert silence. Remove silences whose reason has passed, and ask the owner of any silence without a ticket to add one.
6. Slow lane. Review the merchants in the webhook slow lane. Remove any merchant whose endpoint has answered normally for 24 hours, with `railctl webhooks slow-lane remove`.

Write "daily checks done" in the Rail channel with anything you found. If you skip a day because of an incident, say so, and the next primary does the checks first thing.

## Reconciliation

Reconciliation compares what Rail recorded with what the acquirers report. It runs every night after settlement, owned by the Payments Ledger team. On-call does not run it, but its results often explain alerts and merchant complaints, so it helps to know how to read them.

The report has three sections. Matched lines need no action. Unmatched captures are captures Rail recorded that the acquirer has not reported, or the other way round. Amount mismatches are captures where Rail and the acquirer disagree on the amount, almost always because of currency conversion on the acquirer's side.

An unmatched capture from yesterday is usually just late. The acquirer reports some captures a day after they settle, especially captures made between 23:00 and 01:30 UTC. An unmatched capture older than 2 days is a real problem, and an unmatched capture that Rail does not know about but the acquirer does is the worst kind, since it means someone charged a card outside Rail. That case goes straight to the Payments Ledger team and the engineering manager on call as a SEV2.

Amount mismatches under 0.5% of the payment are expected for payments in another currency and are not reported. Mismatches above that go to the Payments Ledger team as a ticket, not as an incident, unless more than 100 appear in one night.

## Glossary

- Acquirer: the bank or payment company that processes card payments for our merchants and connects to the card networks. Rail uses Northgate and Coralpay.
- Authorization: the request that checks a card can pay and reserves the amount. No money moves yet.
- Capture: the request that turns an authorization into a charge. This is when the cardholder is actually charged.
- Cascading: retrying a declined payment on a second acquirer. Restricted by our contracts.
- Detokenization: swapping a stored token back for the card number before sending a payment to the acquirer. Only the vault does this.
- Network token: a token issued by the card network that the acquirer accepts directly. Payments with network tokens do not need our vault.
- Pinned merchant: a merchant whose payments always go to one acquirer.
- Settlement: the nightly process in which we tell each acquirer which captures and refunds to move money for.
- Slow lane: a separate set of webhook workers for merchants whose endpoints are slow or failing.
- Void: cancelling an authorization before it is captured. No money moves.

## Notes for new on-call engineers {agent=skip}

These notes are for people joining the rota, not for the on-call tools. They collect advice from people who have done many shifts.

Your first few pages will feel bigger than they are. Most Rail alerts resolve themselves or turn out to be a bank or an acquirer having a bad few minutes. Acknowledge, open the dashboard, and give yourself five minutes to look before deciding anything. Nobody expects you to have a theory in the first minute.

Ask for help early. The secondary is there for that, and so is the engineering manager on call. People on the rota would much rather be woken for something that turns out to be nothing than read about a long night the next morning.

Write as you go. Short messages in the incident channel ("looking at Coralpay timeouts in West", "restarting connector pod 3 of 5") help the people who join later and help you write the postmortem. They also stop two people from doing the same thing at once.

Shadow shifts are the best way to learn. During your two shadow shifts, follow every page the primary gets, even the small ones, and ask them to talk through what they are looking at. Most of what is in this runbook makes more sense after you have seen it used.

Take the time back. After a night with more than an hour of pages, you are expected to start late or take the afternoon off. Tell your team lead, and they will move your other work.

If you are going to be out of reach for any part of your shift, even for an hour, arrange cover with the secondary and write it in the rota channel. The two incidents that created the secondary role both started with someone who expected to be reachable and was not.

## Background {agent=skip}

This section explains how Rail and its on-call came to be the way they are. It is for people who are new to the team or who want the history behind a rule. Nothing in it is needed to handle a page.

Paywise started in 2019 as a checkout plugin for small online shops, and in its first two years it did not process payments itself. Shops brought their own payment provider, and the plugin passed payment details through. The first version of Rail was built in 2021, when Paywise signed its first acquirer agreement with Northgate and started offering payments as part of the plugin. That first version was a single service with a database, running in one region, and it handled about 30 requests per second at its peak. On-call was whoever had written the code that broke.

The second acquirer, Coralpay, came in 2022. The main reason was resilience after a Northgate outage in the spring of that year, which stopped every Paywise payment for 5 hours on a Saturday. Adding Coralpay meant adding the router, and with it the routing weights and the first version of failover, which at the time was a person changing a configuration file and redeploying. That process took about 25 minutes on a good night.

The token vault was split out in 2023, as part of the work to reduce the scope of our card data compliance audit. Before that, card numbers were encrypted inside the main Rail database, which put every Rail engineer and every Rail server in scope. The split cut the audit scope to the vault cluster and the vault team, and it is the reason the vault is the one component on-call cannot touch. The vault was placed in West because that was the only region at the time, and moving or doubling it has been discussed every year since.

The Central region went live in 2024. Before that, a regional problem in West meant a full outage. The move to two active regions took nine months, mostly because of the ledger, which had to learn to accept writes from both regions without double-counting.

A formal on-call rota started in early 2023, with six engineers taking a week each. Before that, the Rail Core lead was paged for everything, and in 2022 he was woken on average three nights a week. The secondary role was added in 2024, after two incidents where the primary was unreachable for over 30 minutes, once because of a dead phone battery and once because of a flight that nobody had noticed on the rota. The rule that the outgoing primary stays on until the handover call has happened came from the second one.

Payments Reliability was formed in 2025 as a small team of four, to own this runbook, the alerts, the postmortem process and the on-call tooling. Before that, each component team kept its own notes, and the playbooks for the same alert disagreed. Merging them into this runbook took most of the first half of 2025, and the safety rules section was the part that needed the most arguing.

The on-call group today has 14 engineers from Rail Core, Rail Integrations and Payments Ledger, so each person is primary about once every 14 weeks and secondary about as often. New members shadow two shifts before their first one as secondary, and serve as secondary twice before their first shift as primary.

## Notable incidents {agent=skip}

Short summaries of incidents that shaped this runbook. They are kept for context and for training. The full postmortems are on the Payments Reliability wiki. The procedures in them are as they were at the time, and several have changed since, so do not follow them.

### March 2025, duplicate captures after a replay

A database failover in West left about 2,300 captures in an unknown state for 40 minutes. The on-call engineer at the time found a script in the Rail Core repository that replayed captures from a list, checked that it had been used before, and ran it on the 2,300 payments. About half of those captures had in fact reached the acquirer the first time. The replay sent them again with new idempotency keys, and 1,140 cardholders were charged twice. Finance operations and the Payments Ledger team spent four weeks refunding them and handling the related disputes.

What changed: the safety rule against replaying captures, refunds and payouts, the removal of the replay script, the `DuplicateCaptureSuspected` alert, and the rule that this alert can never be silenced. The engineer who ran the script helped write the safety rule, and is still on the rota.

### October 2025, fraud scoring down overnight

A certificate on the fraud scoring service expired at 23:10 UTC on a Saturday. Every scoring call failed, and the router waited for the full 120 ms timeout on each payment before applying fallback rules. Payments kept working but slowly, and three merchants whose fallback rule was "decline all" declined every payment for 3 hours. Risk Engineering had no out-of-hours rota, and the Rail primary was not allowed to change anything on the fraud side, so the incident lasted until a Risk Engineering lead saw the messages on Sunday morning.

What changed: the fraud fail-open decision, the fail-open command, and the start of the discussion about a Risk Engineering rota, which is still open.

### January 2026, link between regions down

A configuration change at our cloud provider broke the private link between West and Central for 52 minutes. Central could not reach the vault, and every card payment for Central merchants that needed detokenization failed. The on-call engineer moved Central merchants to West, which fixed card payments within 15 minutes but overloaded West's edge, since it was the first Monday after the holidays and traffic was high. The second step of the move was done in one jump of 50% instead of 25%.

What changed: the evacuation steps of 25% with 3 minutes between them, the headroom check before evacuating, and the proposal for a second vault, which was rejected for 2026.

### February 2026, Northgate partial outage and failover flapping

Northgate had a partial outage for about 2 hours on a Friday evening, with error rates going between 3% and 9%. Automatic failover moved traffic to Coralpay when the error rate passed 5%, moved it back when it fell under 1% for 15 minutes, and moved it again when it rose. It flapped four times. Each move restarted the fraud warm-up at the receiving acquirer, and declines at Coralpay doubled for an hour after each move. Coralpay also hit its 1,500 requests per second limit twice, and the router rejected about 31,000 payments with `acquirer_capacity`. The primary set a routing hold after the third flap, which stopped the moves but also left traffic on the failing acquirer.

What changed: the decision to make failover manual, the 30-point limit on weight changes, and the removal of the automatic failover code, which was finished in June.

### August 2026, webhook backlog from one merchant

A large merchant's webhook endpoint started answering every request after 29 seconds, just under our 30-second timeout, for a whole afternoon. The dispatcher's workers spent most of their time waiting on that one endpoint, and the backlog for every other merchant grew to 1.4 million webhooks and 3 hours of delay. Merchants who depend on webhooks to release orders could not ship.

What changed: the slow lane, the `slow-lane` commands, and the step in the webhook backlog playbook that checks for one merchant first.

## Review meeting notes (2026-10-08) {agent=skip}

Notes from the quarterly runbook review, for the people on the rota who could not attend. Present were Kofi Annerud, Tomas Veidt, Amara Quist, Lena Marsh, Priya Nandakumar and two engineers from Rail Integrations.

- Kofi went through the follow-ups. The automatic failover code is gone from the router since June, so the routing hold flag only matters for manual weight changes now. Tomas will check whether any dashboards still show the old failover state panel.
- Amara raised bank transfers again. Finance operations finds stuck transfers before we do, every time. The alert is on the follow-up list, due mid-November.
- Lena asked whether 14 days is enough warning for client certificates, given that Coralpay needed 19 days last time to accept a new one. Agreed to move to 30 days.
- Long discussion about bulk refunds. Finance operations sent a written request before the meeting. Payments Ledger still says no without automatic checks against the capture report. No decision. It goes to the January operations review, and the current rule stays.
- Priya asked for a disputes playbook. Chargeback spikes have paged people twice this quarter with no playbook. She will write the first version.
- The group agreed to run a tabletop exercise on a West vault outage in November, before the freeze, since the January incident showed that most of the rota had never thought through what evacuation means without a vault.
- Next review in January 2027, after the freeze.

## Runbook changes {agent=skip}

A record of changes to this runbook, newest first. It is kept for reviewers and auditors.

- 2026-10-08: quarterly review. Added the ledger writer lagging and both acquirers failing playbooks, the daily checks, and the limits reference. Merged the old "payment states" page into this runbook.
- 2026-08-27: added the webhook slow lane after the August incident.
- 2026-07-15: quarterly review. Rewrote the incident roles section. Moved the acquirer contact sheet out of the wiki into the password manager.
- 2026-04-20: decisions section updated for manual acquirer failover.
- 2026-02-16: evacuation steps changed to 25% with 3 minutes between steps after the January incident. Added the second vault proposal to the decisions section as rejected.
- 2026-01-12: quarterly review. Added the change freeze dates for 2026 to 2027.
- 2025-11-03: added fraud fail-open.
- 2025-04-02: added the safety rules section and the duplicate capture playbook after the March incident.
- 2025-01-20: first version, merged from the component teams' notes by Payments Reliability.
